1. Overview
------------

The test-speed.sh script measures the performance impact of the OTEL filter on
HAProxy at various rate-limit settings.  For each test configuration, the script
iterates through a series of rate-limit values -- from full tracing (100%) down
to the filter being completely removed -- measuring throughput and latency at
each level.

The script backs up the live configuration files in each test directory to
.orig copies (haproxy.cfg.orig, otel.cfg.orig and otel.yml.orig) and then
regenerates the live files for each run by applying a sed substitution to those
.orig backups.  The substitution adjusts the rate-limit value (or disables or
removes the filter) before each run.

In every run the otel.yml signal exporters are additionally rewired to their
dev_null variants (ostream exporters writing to /dev/null), so the published
numbers measure the filter's processing cost without the cost of a live OTLP
export.


2. Prerequisites
-----------------

The script supports the following HTTP servers as the backend origin server
on port 8000:

  - haterm : HAProxy's dummy HTTP server for benchmarks, preferred when
             available.  It is built from the HAProxy source tree with
             the command 'make TARGET=linux-glibc haterm' (see
             doc/haterm.txt there) and is searched for in PATH and then
             at ../../haproxy/haterm.  Only a build with thread support
             is usable: a build without USE_THREAD hangs at startup
             before binding its listening address.

  - thttpd : a lightweight single-threaded HTTP server, used when haterm
             is not found or when selected with the '-b thttpd' option.
             It must be available in PATH and serves the small static
             HTML file index.html.

The following tool must be installed and available in PATH:

  - wrk    : an HTTP benchmarking tool that generates the test load.
             See https://github.com/wg/wrk

An optional tool is used when present:

  - socat  : queries the 'flt-otel scopes', 'flt-otel instruments' and
             'flt-otel status' CLI commands before each HAProxy stop; the
             output is appended to the per-level HAProxy log described in
             section 3.  Without socat the dump is silently skipped.


3. Running the test
--------------------

The test is executed from the test directory.  It can be run for all
configurations at once or for a single configuration.

To run all configurations:

  % ./test-speed.sh all

This produces result files in the _logs directory:

  _logs/README-speed-fe-be
  _logs/README-speed-sa
  _logs/README-speed-cmp
  _logs/README-speed-ctx
  _logs/README-speed-full
  _logs/README-speed-tcp
  _logs/README-speed-updown

To run a single configuration:

  % ./test-speed.sh <cfg> [<dir>]

Where <cfg> corresponds to a run-<cfg>.sh script and <dir> is the configuration
directory (defaults to <cfg>).  For example:

  % ./test-speed.sh sa
  % ./test-speed.sh fe-be fe
  % ./test-speed.sh cmp
  % ./test-speed.sh ctx

The '-b <backend>' option selects the backend origin server, either by a name
resolved through PATH or by an explicit path to the executable; without the
option, haterm is preferred and thttpd is the fallback.  The '-d <duration>'
option overrides the test duration (in seconds), while the '-r <rate-limits>'
option replaces the default rate-limit series described in section 5 with the
given space-separated list.  Each result file records the used backend on its
first line.

Each rate-limit level also writes the HAProxy output of its run to the log file
_logs/_log-haproxy-<cfg>-<ratio>; the fe-be configuration logs its instances
separately, with the -fe and -be suffixes appended to that name.  Before the
instance is stopped, the output of the 'flt-otel scopes', 'flt-otel instruments'
and 'flt-otel status' CLI commands is appended to the log, preserving the filter
state and counters of the finished level.


4. Test parameters
-------------------

The wrk benchmarking tool is invoked with load parameters that depend on the
selected backend:

  -t8 -c256  8 threads and 256 concurrent connections (haterm backend)
  -t4 -c64   4 threads and 64 concurrent connections (thttpd backend)
  -d300      5-minute test duration (300 seconds; the script's '-d' option
             overrides it)
  --latency  latency distribution reporting

Each rate-limit level is tested sequentially.  Between runs, HAProxy is stopped
via SIGUSR1 and restarted with the next rate-limit configuration.  A 10-second
pause separates consecutive runs.

The requested URL is /index.html?s=44 and produces a 44-byte response body from
either backend: haterm generates the body from the 's' query parameter, while
thttpd ignores the query string and serves the 44-byte static file index.html.
HAProxy listens on port 10080 and proxies the requests to the origin server on
port 8000.

haterm is the HAProxy engine itself acting as a dummy origin server.  Being
multi-threaded and event-driven, its capacity (about 750,000 req/s measured
directly on the reference machine) sits far above what the proxied setup can
deliver, so the HAProxy instance under test remains the bottleneck at every
rate-limit level.  Saturating such a fast backend requires a high connection
concurrency, hence the -c256 default.  The 'off' baseline thereby becomes the
real ceiling, at about 300,000 req/s for the standalone (sa) configuration,
and the filter measures as a genuine cost below it.  Since haterm keeps its
connections alive, even the tcp configuration works over long-lived tunnels
rather than opening a fresh connection per request.

thttpd is single-threaded: it saturates one core at roughly 45,000 to 49,000
proxied requests per second, and every measurement is compressed against that
backend ceiling.  Raising the connection count only queues requests in front of
its single thread and lowers the throughput, hence the reduced -t4 -c64 load
parameters.  Because the baseline is backend-bound, rate-limit levels whose
cost exceeds the remaining headroom flatten near the ceiling, and the computed
overheads understate the filter's cost relative to an unconstrained baseline.
The thttpd backend is kept for comparison with results collected before haterm
was introduced.

All published figures were measured on the following test system:

  - CPU     : AMD Ryzen 7 8845HS (8 cores, 16 threads, max 5.1 GHz)
  - memory  : 96 GiB
  - OS      : Linux, kernel 6.17.0
  - HAProxy : version 3.5-dev2, used for both the tested instance and the
              haterm origin server
  - wrk     : version 4.1.0


5. Rate-limit levels
---------------------

By default the script tests nine rate-limit levels in the following order (the
'-r' option substitutes another series):

  100.0    - the filter processes every stream (worst case)
   75.0    - the filter processes 75% of streams
   50.0    - the filter processes 50% of streams
   25.0    - the filter processes 25% of streams
   10.0    - the filter processes 10% of streams
    2.5    - the filter processes 2.5% of streams
    0.0    - the filter is loaded and attached to every stream but never
             processes any telemetry (the rate-limit check always fails);
             this measures the per-stream attach and detach overhead

  disabled - the filter is loaded but disabled via 'option disabled'; it is not
             attached to streams at all; this measures the cost of loading and
             initializing the filter library without any per-stream work

  off      - the 'filter opentelemetry' and 'otel-group' directives are
             commented out of haproxy.cfg; the filter is not loaded and has zero
             presence in the processing path; this is the absolute baseline

In the result tables, the 'overhead' column is the throughput loss relative to
the 'off' baseline, expressed as a percentage:

  overhead = (req/s_off - req/s_test) / req/s_off * 100


6. Test configurations
-----------------------

The following OTEL filter configurations are tested.  They differ in complexity
and in the features they exercise:

  sa     - Standalone.  Exercises most of the HAProxy filter events with spans,
           attributes, events, links, baggage, status, metrics and groups (the
           exact coverage is listed in README-sa) and is the most comprehensive
           benchmarked configuration, representing the worst-case scenario.

  cmp    - Comparison.  A simplified configuration made for comparison with
           other tracing implementations.  It uses a reduced span hierarchy
           without context propagation, groups or metrics.  This is closer to
           a typical production deployment.

  ctx    - Context propagation.  Similar to 'sa' in scope coverage, but spans
           are opened using extracted span contexts (inject/extract via HAProxy
           variables) as parent references instead of direct span names.  This
           adds the overhead of context serialization, variable storage and
           deserialization on every scope execution.

  fe-be  - Frontend / backend.  Two cascaded HAProxy instances: the frontend
           (fe) creates the root trace and injects span context into HTTP
           headers; the backend (be) extracts the context and continues the
           trace.  This configuration measures the combined overhead of two
           OTEL filter instances plus the inter-process context propagation
           cost.

           Note: the rate-limit is varied only on the frontend.  The backend
           always runs with its default configuration (filter enabled,
           hard-errors on).  The backend configuration is only modified for
           the 'disabled' and 'off' levels.

  full   - Full event coverage.  Extends 'sa' with the remaining lifecycle
           events, covering every filter event except on-http-tarpit-request.

  tcp    - TCP mode.  Traces the raw connection on a 'mode tcp' proxy with a
           single session span and counts the forwarded payload.  The span
           opens once per connection and, with the keep-alive haterm backend,
           is amortized over all the requests a connection carries; see the
           results in section 7.6.

  updown - Up-down counter.  A metrics-only configuration: a udcnt_int
           instrument records a signed per-session delta on client session
           start and end, with no spans or context propagation.


7. Results
-----------

The tables below summarize the benchmarking results.  The 'req/s' column shows
the sustained request rate reported by wrk, 'avg latency' is the average
response time, and 'overhead' is the throughput loss relative to the 'off'
baseline.  A table is published for every benchmarked configuration; values
inside the run-to-run scatter are shown as '~0'.

The tables were collected with the haterm backend, using the -t8 -c256 load
parameters and 5-minute runs on a fully saturated 16-CPU machine.  They are
not comparable with the results collected earlier through the thttpd backend,
whose single-threaded ceiling compressed all measurements and understated the
filter's cost.  Note that these are saturation figures: the overhead states
how much of the peak request rate full-load tracing consumes, and it exceeds
the impact seen at traffic levels below the capacity ceiling.

Keep in mind that these configurations are stress tests rather than deployment
templates: almost all of them produce a deliberately excessive signal volume.
The sa, full and ctx configurations open several spans with attributes, events,
links, baggage and metrics on nearly every filter event of every stream, which
no real deployment has any reason to do.  A production setup would typically
record a single span pair around the request and the response with a handful
of attributes -- less work per stream than even the cmp configuration -- and it
would then sample with a modest rate-limit, keeping the real cost far below
the published figures.


7.1. Standalone (sa)
---------------------

  ---------------------------------------------------------------
   rate-limit     req/s     avg latency     overhead
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
      100.0%     68,686         3.73 ms        77.3%
       75.0%     81,488         3.17 ms        73.0%
       50.0%    107,213         2.44 ms        64.5%
       25.0%    149,747         1.84 ms        50.4%
       10.0%    205,090         1.42 ms        32.1%
        2.5%    266,251         1.01 ms        11.9%
        0.0%    299,388         0.88 ms         0.9%
     disabled   298,250         0.88 ms         1.3%
     off        302,051         0.87 ms     baseline
  ---------------------------------------------------------------

With its whole event set active, the sa configuration at 100% rate-limit costs
77% of the peak request rate, and the average latency rises from 0.87 ms to
3.73 ms.  The cost falls steadily with the rate-limit -- 32% at 10% and 12% at
2.5% -- while the '0.0' and 'disabled' levels stay within about one percent of
the baseline.


7.2. Comparison (cmp)
----------------------

  ---------------------------------------------------------------
   rate-limit     req/s     avg latency     overhead
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
      100.0%    143,291         1.80 ms        53.0%
       75.0%    166,504         1.59 ms        45.4%
       50.0%    195,512         1.41 ms        35.9%
       25.0%    230,052         1.22 ms        24.6%
       10.0%    262,225         1.04 ms        14.1%
        2.5%    291,758         0.91 ms         4.4%
        0.0%    302,844         0.86 ms         0.8%
     disabled   304,185         0.86 ms           ~0
     off        305,166         0.86 ms     baseline
  ---------------------------------------------------------------

The simplified cmp configuration shows significantly lower overhead than sa at
every rate-limit level: 53% at 100% rate-limit, 14% at 10% and 4.4% at 2.5%.
Attaching the filter without processing any telemetry ('0.0') costs 0.8%, and
the 'disabled' level is indistinguishable from the baseline.


7.3. Context propagation (ctx)
-------------------------------

  ---------------------------------------------------------------
   rate-limit     req/s     avg latency     overhead
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
      100.0%     49,998         5.12 ms        83.6%
       75.0%     62,079         4.12 ms        79.6%
       50.0%     82,588         3.10 ms        72.9%
       25.0%    123,494         2.11 ms        59.4%
       10.0%    185,332         1.49 ms        39.1%
        2.5%    258,145         1.07 ms        15.2%
        0.0%    301,631         0.87 ms         0.9%
     disabled   302,066         0.87 ms         0.8%
     off        304,454         0.86 ms     baseline
  ---------------------------------------------------------------

The ctx configuration is the most expensive due to the inject/extract cycle on
every scope execution: 84% at 100% rate-limit and still 39% at 10%.  Only near
the 2.5% rate-limit does the cost fall toward single digits, while the filter
attachment overhead ('0.0') remains at 0.9%.


7.4. Frontend / backend (fe-be)
--------------------------------

  ---------------------------------------------------------------
   rate-limit     req/s     avg latency     overhead
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
      100.0%     67,719         3.79 ms        62.9%
       75.0%     77,117         3.32 ms        57.8%
       50.0%     90,086         2.84 ms        50.7%
       25.0%    112,488         2.27 ms        38.4%
       10.0%    137,040         1.86 ms        25.0%
        2.5%    156,012         1.64 ms        14.6%
        0.0%    166,262         1.53 ms         9.0%
     disabled   181,294         1.40 ms         0.7%
     off        182,638         1.39 ms     baseline
  ---------------------------------------------------------------

The fe-be configuration involves two HAProxy instances in series, so the
absolute baseline (off) is already lower at 182,638 req/s due to the extra
network hop.  The rate-limit is varied only on the frontend; the backend
always has the filter loaded with hard-errors enabled.

This explains the 9.0% overhead at rate-limit 0.0: even though the frontend
never traces, the backend filter still attaches to every stream, attempts to
extract context from the HTTP headers, fails (because the frontend did not
inject any context), and the hard-errors option stops further processing.
This per-stream attach/extract/error cycle accounts for the residual cost.

When both instances have the filter disabled (disabled level), the overhead
drops to 0.7%, consistent with the single-instance configurations.


7.5. Full event coverage (full)
--------------------------------

  ---------------------------------------------------------------
   rate-limit     req/s     avg latency     overhead
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
      100.0%     52,616         4.86 ms        82.8%
       75.0%     63,738         4.01 ms        79.1%
       50.0%     85,361         3.02 ms        72.0%
       25.0%    128,246         2.07 ms        58.0%
       10.0%    184,966         1.50 ms        39.4%
        2.5%    251,750         1.10 ms        17.5%
        0.0%    299,886         0.88 ms         1.7%
     disabled   301,403         0.87 ms         1.2%
     off        305,206         0.86 ms     baseline
  ---------------------------------------------------------------

The full configuration extends sa with the remaining lifecycle events, and it
costs correspondingly more at every level: 83% at 100% rate-limit, 39% at 10%
and 18% at 2.5%, close to the ctx figures.  The attachment overhead ('0.0') of
1.7% is the largest among the single-instance configurations, and it matches
the larger per-stream event set.


7.6. TCP mode (tcp)
--------------------

  ---------------------------------------------------------------
   rate-limit     req/s     avg latency     overhead
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
      100.0%    336,033       721.91 us         5.1%
       75.0%    345,857       702.26 us         2.4%
       50.0%    343,517       706.68 us         3.0%
       25.0%    357,053       662.83 us        -0.8%
       10.0%    351,205       686.46 us         0.8%
        2.5%    356,290       682.02 us        -0.6%
        0.0%    364,624       651.41 us        -2.9%
     disabled   369,502       634.14 us        -4.3%
     off        354,184       676.34 us     baseline
  ---------------------------------------------------------------

The tcp configuration has the highest absolute baseline (354,184 req/s), as a
'mode tcp' proxy performs no HTTP processing.  Its single session span opens
once per connection, and because the haterm backend keeps connections alive,
that work is amortized over the whole keep-alive tunnel.  The measurable cost
is therefore about 5% at 100% rate-limit, with all the other levels inside the
run-to-run scatter -- including readings nominally faster than the baseline,
which appear as negative overheads in the table.


7.7. Up-down counter (updown)
------------------------------

  ---------------------------------------------------------------
   rate-limit     req/s     avg latency     overhead
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
      100.0%    261,685         1.10 ms        13.8%
       75.0%    270,673         1.02 ms        10.8%
       50.0%    278,482         0.98 ms         8.2%
       25.0%    287,652         0.94 ms         5.2%
       10.0%    293,626         0.91 ms         3.2%
        2.5%    300,193         0.88 ms         1.1%
        0.0%    302,066         0.87 ms           ~0
     disabled   303,746         0.86 ms           ~0
     off        303,479         0.86 ms     baseline
  ---------------------------------------------------------------

The metrics-only updown configuration is the cheapest HTTP-mode setup: 14% at
100% rate-limit, 3.2% at 10% and about 1% at 2.5%.  A single up-down counter
instrument without spans or context propagation leaves the request path almost
untouched at the lower sampling rates.


8. Summary
-----------

The table below shows each benchmarked configuration's overhead at selected
rate-limit levels:

  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   rate-limit       sa      cmp      ctx    fe-be     full      tcp   updown
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
      100.0%    77.3%    53.0%    83.6%    62.9%    82.8%     5.1%    13.8%
       25.0%    50.4%    24.6%    59.4%    38.4%    58.0%       ~0     5.2%
       10.0%    32.1%    14.1%    39.1%    25.0%    39.4%       ~0     3.2%
        2.5%    11.9%     4.4%    15.2%    14.6%    17.5%       ~0     1.1%
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------

Key observations:

  - Full tracing is expensive under saturation: depending on the configuration,
    the 100% rate-limit costs between 53% and 84% of the peak request rate on
    an HTTP proxy.

  - The overhead falls with the rate-limit, but more slowly than the rate
    itself: at a 10% rate-limit the sa configuration still loses 32% of its
    peak rate.  The rate-limit budgets the tracing work, it does not scale
    the cost proportionally.

  - The cmp configuration, which uses a reduced span hierarchy typical of
    production deployments, is the cheapest span-producing HTTP setup at
    every rate-limit level.

  - The ctx and full configurations are the heaviest: context inject/extract
    on every scope and the extended event set each add a comparable surcharge
    over sa.

  - The fe-be configuration keeps a 9% residual cost even at rate-limit 0.0
    because the backend instance attaches, attempts a context extract and
    fails on every stream regardless of the frontend rate-limit.

  - The tcp configuration's session span is amortized over the keep-alive
    connections, so its cost stays inside the measurement scatter at all but
    the highest rate-limits.

  - The metrics-only updown configuration costs 13.8% at full sampling and
    about 3% at a 10% rate-limit.

  - Attaching the filter without any telemetry work (rate-limit 0.0) costs at
    most 1.7%, and the 'disabled' level at most 1.3%, in any single-instance
    configuration.

  - These are saturation figures from a fully loaded machine.  A deployment
    running below the capacity ceiling mainly pays the per-request latency
    cost instead, e.g. 0.87 ms against 3.73 ms at 100% rate-limit in the sa
    configuration.

  - The benchmarked configurations are intentionally signal-heavy stress
    tests, not deployment templates.  A production configuration records
    much less telemetry per stream and, combined with a modest rate-limit,
    stays far below these overheads.
